Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether there exists a positive function $u$ on a domain $D \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ with $\liminf_{y\to x} u(y) = 0$ for all $x \in D$, yet $u \neq 0$ a.e.

**Answer: Yes.**

**Construction.** Let $D \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ ($n \geq 1$) be any domain, and let $\{q_k\}_{k=1}^\infty$ be a countable dense subset of $D$ (for instance, $D \cap \mathbb{Q}^n$). Define

$$
u(x) = \begin{cases} 2^{-k} & \text{if } x = q_k \text{ for some } k \geq 1, \\ 1 & \text{if } x \notin \{q_k : k \geq 1\}. \end{cases}
$$

**$u > 0$ everywhere.** If $x = q_k$, then $u(x) = 2^{-k} > 0$. If $x \notin \{q_k\}$, then $u(x) = 1 > 0$. Since every $x \in D$ falls into one of these two cases, $u > 0$ on all of $D$.

**$\liminf_{y \to x} u(y) = 0$ for every $x \in D$.** Fix $x \in D$ and $\varepsilon > 0$. Choose $N$ large enough that $2^{-N} < \varepsilon$. I claim the set $\{q_k : k \geq N\}$ is still dense in $D$. Indeed, $D$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^n$, so every open ball $B \subset D$ is uncountable and contains infinitely many points of $\{q_k\}$ (since $\{q_k\}$ is dense in $D$, its intersection with any open subset of $D$ is infinite). Removing the finitely many points $\{q_1, \dots, q_{N-1}\}$ leaves a nonempty (indeed infinite) intersection with every such ball, so $\{q_k : k \geq N\}$ is dense in $D$.

Therefore, there exists a sequence $(q_{k_j})$ with $k_j \geq N$ and $q_{k_j} \to x$. Then

$$
u(q_{k_j}) = 2^{-k_j} \leq 2^{-N} < \varepsilon.
$$

Since $u \geq 0$, we get $\liminf_{y \to x} u(y) \leq \lim_{j \to \infty} u(q_{k_j}) \leq 2^{-N} < \varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, $\liminf_{y \to x} u(y) = 0$.

**$u \neq 0$ almost everywhere.** Since $u > 0$ everywhere, the set $\{x \in D : u(x) = 0\} = \emptyset$, which has Lebesgue measure $0$. Equivalently, $\{x \in D : u(x) \neq 0\} = D$ has full measure. In fact, since $\{q_k\}$ is countable (hence measure zero), $u = 1$ on $D \setminus \{q_k\}$, so $u = 1$ a.e.

**Conclusion.** The function $u$ is strictly positive on $D$, satisfies $\liminf_{y \to x} u(y) = 0$ at every point $x \in D$, and is nonzero (indeed equal to $1$) almost everywhere. Such a function exists. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** The construction critically relies on $u$ being discontinuous. If $u$ were required to be lower semicontinuous (e.g., superharmonic), then $u(x) \leq \liminf_{y \to x} u(y) = 0$ would contradict $u > 0$, so no such function would exist. The answer is **yes** for arbitrary functions, **no** under lower semicontinuity.

